          "title": "Quantization noise",
          "goal": "Why each bit of a quantizer buys about 6 dB, and where the textbook formula is optimistic.",
          "text": "A quantizer's error is a sawtooth one step high, and its power is the step squared over 12. Against a full-scale sine that gives 6.02 N + 1.76 dB, about 74.0 dB at 12 bits. The spec signals.t27 also measures it: it quantizes a real sine over 4096 samples holding 127 periods and divides the powers. At 12 bits the two agree; at 1 bit the formula claims 7.78 dB and the measurement finds 6.44, because so coarse an error is not uniform.",
