          "goal": "Why a decimation-in-time FFT takes its input in bit-reversed order, and where the twiddles come from.",
          "text": "Split the input into even and odd samples, then split each half again, and the order you end up reading them in is the index with its bits reversed. At 8 points, index 1 is 001 and goes to place 4, and 3 is 011 and goes to place 6. Reversing twice gives the index back, so the reordering is a set of swaps that needs no arithmetic, only wiring. The twiddles W are points on the unit circle, cos minus j sin. The spec signals.t27 computes both.",
